For those blissfully unaware, the source code for popular coding agent Claude Code got leaked in March of this year because a JavaScript source map was accidentally published to their npm package (whoops). Enclosed in this file was a reference to a storage bucket containing over 500,000 lines of unobfuscated code, revealing in broad detail the inner workings of Claude Code.

Amongst everything I found in the codebase, this gem of a function stood out to me the most:

```TypeScript
export function matchesNegativeKeyword(input: string): boolean {
    const lowerInput = input.toLowerCase()
    const negativePattern =
        /\b(wtf|wth|ffs|omfg|shit(ty|tiest)?|dumbass|horrible|awful|piss(ed|ing)? off|piece of (shit|crap|junk)|what the (fuck|hell)|fucking? (broken|useless|terrible|awful|horrible)|fuck you|screw (this|you)|so frustrating|this sucks|damn it)\b/

    return negativePattern.test(lowerInput)
}
```

I have unofficially dubbed this: **The F\*\*\* Index&trade;**

Claude Code can navigate repositories, edit files, run commands, commission large scale workflows, summon sub-agents, and yet, somewhere inside all of that is a small utility trying to answer a much older engineering question:

 > Did the user just swear at us?

Agent performance telemetry via regex-based sentiment analysis is a sentence I never thought I would say, and yet here we are.

Even more surprisingly, this approach kind of makes sense.

The actual implementation is quite interesting, because it leads into a problem that every AI product eventually encounters: how do you know whether the AI is doing a good job?


## Does This Mean I Can't Swear at Claude Anymore?

No. By all means, continue to swear your heart out.

The sole purpose of this function is to capture and log user satisfaction analytics. The function itself returns a boolean and is tied to an analytics event:

```TypeScript
    const isNegative = matchesNegativeKeyword(userPromptText)
    const isKeepGoing = matchesKeepGoingKeyword(userPromptText)

    logEvent('tengu_input_prompt', {
        is_negative: isNegative,
        is_keep_going: isKeepGoing,
    })
```

The exact profanity used doesn't even matter as it gets reduced down to a `true`/`false` value by the time it gets logged. This whole flow is almost weirdly low-tech given who this codebase belongs to.

